## What happened

**Apple removed DSA from its Security/CommonCrypto frameworks.** .NET on macOS delegates to OS-native crypto libraries, so `DSA.Create()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS. (On Linux it uses OpenSSL, which still supports DSA, and on Windows it uses CNG — both still work.)

This is a known platform gap that .NET surfaces at runtime rather than compile time.

## How to fix it

You have two practical options:

### Option 1 — Use BouncyCastle (recommended for legacy verification)

```csharp
// dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

public bool VerifyDsaSignature(byte[] message, byte[] signature, AsymmetricKeyParameter publicKey)
{
    var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA1withDSA");
    verifier.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);
    verifier.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    return verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

This is pure managed code — no OS crypto dependency, works everywhere.

### Option 2 — Migrate to a modern algorithm

If you control both signing and verification, replace DSA with **ECDSA** or **Ed25519**, which are supported on all three platforms:

```csharp
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create(ECCurve.NamedCurves.nistP256);
```

## Recommendation

If you only need to **verify** existing legacy signatures (you're not creating new ones), go with **BouncyCastle**. If you can rotate keys, migrate to ECDSA/EdDSA and drop DSA entirely.
